The queue here stores words, not whole paths. During BFS, each word keeps a list of its possible parents from the previous level. After the shortest level is found, a small DFS walks through those parents to build the answers.

The wildcard map makes neighbor lookup easier. For example, `hot` creates `*ot`, `h*t`, and `ho*`.

```python
from collections import defaultdict, deque

class Solution:
    def findSequences(self, beginWord, endWord, wordList):
        words = set(wordList)
        if endWord not in words:
            return []
        if beginWord == endWord:
            return [[beginWord]]

        patterns = defaultdict(list)
        all_words = words | {beginWord}
        word_length = len(beginWord)

        for word in all_words:
            for i in range(word_length):
                pattern = word[:i] + "*" + word[i + 1:]
                patterns[pattern].append(word)

        queue = deque([beginWord])
        unused = set(words)
        unused.discard(beginWord)
        parents = defaultdict(list)
        found = False

        while queue and not found:
            level_size = len(queue)
            seen_this_level = set()

            for _ in range(level_size):
                current = queue.popleft()

                for i in range(word_length):
                    pattern = current[:i] + "*" + current[i + 1:]
                    for neighbor in patterns[pattern]:
                        if neighbor not in unused:
                            continue

                        parents[neighbor].append(current)
                        seen_this_level.add(neighbor)
                        if neighbor == endWord:
                            found = True
                        else:
                            queue.append(neighbor)

            unused -= seen_this_level

        if not found:
            return []

        answer = []
        path = [endWord]

        def build_paths(word):
            if word == beginWord:
                answer.append(path[::-1])
                return

            for parent in parents[word]:
                path.append(parent)
                build_paths(parent)
                path.pop()

        build_paths(endWord)
        return answer
```

If `hit` reaches `cog`, the parent list for `cog` can contain both `dog` and `log`. The DFS then follows both choices. Notice that removal from `unused` happens after the whole level, so another word in that same level can still become a parent.

There is no path when `endWord` is missing, and the method returns an empty list in that case.

Time complexity is `O(N * L + output)` when the pattern lists are used, ignoring the fixed alphabet size. Space complexity is `O(N * L + output)`.

This keeps a simple queue of paths, which is easy to check while learning the problem. BFS guarantees that paths are considered by length. When one path reaches the target, the rest of the current level is still processed so other answers of the same length are not lost.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> findSequences(String beginWord, String endWord, List<String> wordList) {
        List<List<String>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Set<String> remaining = new HashSet<>(wordList);

        if (!remaining.contains(endWord)) {
            return result;
        }
        if (beginWord.equals(endWord)) {
            List<String> onlyPath = new ArrayList<>();
            onlyPath.add(beginWord);
            result.add(onlyPath);
            return result;
        }

        remaining.remove(beginWord);
        Queue<List<String>> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        List<String> firstPath = new ArrayList<>();
        firstPath.add(beginWord);
        queue.offer(firstPath);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int levelSize = queue.size();
            Set<String> usedThisLevel = new HashSet<>();
            boolean found = false;

            for (int count = 0; count < levelSize; count++) {
                List<String> path = queue.poll();
                String current = path.get(path.size() - 1);
                char[] letters = current.toCharArray();

                for (int i = 0; i < letters.length; i++) {
                    char original = letters[i];
                    for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {
                        if (ch == original) {
                            continue;
                        }

                        letters[i] = ch;
                        String next = new String(letters);
                        if (!remaining.contains(next)) {
                            continue;
                        }

                        List<String> nextPath = new ArrayList<>(path);
                        nextPath.add(next);
                        usedThisLevel.add(next);

                        if (next.equals(endWord)) {
                            result.add(nextPath);
                            found = true;
                        } else {
                            queue.offer(nextPath);
                        }
                    }
                    letters[i] = original;
                }
            }

            remaining.removeAll(usedThisLevel);
            if (found) {
                break;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

A small dry run is `hit -> hot -> dot -> dog -> cog`. The queue starts with `[hit]`, then changes to `[hit, hot]`, and so on until the target path is added. If `lot` also reaches `log` and then `cog`, that path is collected from the same shortest level.

This version is readable, but copying lists can use a lot of memory when the number of shortest paths is large.

Time complexity is roughly `O(N * L * 26 + output)`. Space complexity is `O(N * L + output)`.

Instead of generating a new word for every character change, this version first builds a graph by comparing every pair of words. Two words get an edge when they differ in exactly one position.

Then BFS starts from `endWord` and stores the shortest distance to every reachable word. The DFS only moves from a word to a neighbor whose distance is one smaller, so every generated path is shortest.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <queue>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
    vector<vector<int>> graph;
    vector<int> distanceFromEnd;
    vector<string> words;
    vector<vector<string>> answer;
    vector<string> path;

    bool differsByOne(const string &a, const string &b) {
        int differences = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(a.size()); ++i) {
            if (a[i] != b[i]) {
                ++differences;
            }
        }
        return differences == 1;
    }

    void collect(int node) {
        path.push_back(words[node]);

        if (words[node] == words[0]) {
            answer.push_back(path);
            path.pop_back();
            return;
        }

        for (int next : graph[node]) {
            if (distanceFromEnd[next] == distanceFromEnd[node] - 1) {
                collect(next);
            }
        }

        path.pop_back();
    }

public:
    vector<vector<string>> findSequences(string beginWord, string endWord,
                                         vector<string> &wordList) {
        answer.clear();
        path.clear();
        words.clear();

        unordered_set<string> uniqueWords(wordList.begin(), wordList.end());
        if (!uniqueWords.count(endWord)) {
            return answer;
        }

        words.push_back(beginWord);
        for (const string &word : wordList) {
            if (word != beginWord && uniqueWords.count(word)) {
                words.push_back(word);
                uniqueWords.erase(word);
            }
        }

        int start = 0;
        int target = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(words.size()); ++i) {
            if (words[i] == endWord) {
                target = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (target == -1) {
            return answer;
        }
        if (beginWord == endWord) {
            return {{beginWord}};
        }

        int n = static_cast<int>(words.size());
        graph.assign(n, {});
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; ++j) {
                if (differsByOne(words[i], words[j])) {
                    graph[i].push_back(j);
                    graph[j].push_back(i);
                }
            }
        }

        distanceFromEnd.assign(n, -1);
        queue<int> queue;
        queue.push(target);
        distanceFromEnd[target] = 0;

        while (!queue.empty()) {
            int current = queue.front();
            queue.pop();

            for (int next : graph[current]) {
                if (distanceFromEnd[next] == -1) {
                    distanceFromEnd[next] = distanceFromEnd[current] + 1;
                    queue.push(next);
                }
            }
        }

        if (distanceFromEnd[start] == -1) {
            return answer;
        }

        collect(start);
        return answer;
    }
};
```

An empty answer is returned when the target is not in the list or cannot be reached. The input list is not changed.

The pairwise graph construction is easy to understand, but it is slower than trying character replacements when the word list is large. Its time complexity is `O(N^2 * L)`, plus the time needed to output all paths. Space complexity is `O(N^2 + output)` for the graph and results.

Kept the first version very direct: the BFS queue stores complete paths instead of only words. Since BFS works level by level, the first level that reaches `endWord` contains all shortest answers.

Words found during one level are removed only after that level finishes. This matters because two paths from the same level may reach the same next word.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <queue>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<string>> findSequences(string beginWord, string endWord,
                                         vector<string> &wordList) {
        vector<vector<string>> answer;
        unordered_set<string> words(wordList.begin(), wordList.end());

        if (!words.count(endWord)) {
            return answer;
        }
        if (beginWord == endWord) {
            return {{beginWord}};
        }

        words.erase(beginWord);
        queue<vector<string>> paths;
        paths.push({beginWord});

        while (!paths.empty()) {
            int levelSize = static_cast<int>(paths.size());
            unordered_set<string> usedThisLevel;
            bool reachedEnd = false;

            for (int count = 0; count < levelSize; ++count) {
                vector<string> path = paths.front();
                paths.pop();
                string current = path.back();

                for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(current.size()); ++i) {
                    char original = current[i];
                    for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ++ch) {
                        if (ch == original) {
                            continue;
                        }

                        current[i] = ch;
                        if (!words.count(current)) {
                            continue;
                        }

                        vector<string> nextPath = path;
                        nextPath.push_back(current);
                        usedThisLevel.insert(current);

                        if (current == endWord) {
                            answer.push_back(nextPath);
                            reachedEnd = true;
                        } else {
                            paths.push(nextPath);
                        }
                    }
                    current[i] = original;
                }
            }

            for (const string &word : usedThisLevel) {
                words.erase(word);
            }

            if (reachedEnd) {
                break;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

For example, with `hit` to `cog`, the queue eventually creates paths through both `dot` and `lot`, so both shortest sequences are returned. The trade-off is memory: full paths are copied in the queue, which can be large when many paths share words.

Time complexity is about `O(N * L * 26 + output)`, where `N` is the number of words and `L` is the word length. Space complexity is `O(N * L + output)`.

